(16) There is no protector here, neither mother nor father;
we see a wilderness, empty dwellings, without shelter.
(17) There is no water, no trees, and no birds —

(132) Having performed sinful deeds, they fall into the hells;
they eat heated iron and drink molten metal.
(133) Embers rain from their bodies; burned, they cry out terribly;
their bodies burn in this hell of great terror.
(134) They do not know happiness, nor do they understand the <s>Dharma</s>;
the fool wanders through unrighteousness and does not obtain any happiness.
(135) Endowed with faith and moral conduct, possessed of wisdom, of great austerity,
he serves a virtuous friend and quickly becomes a <s>Tathāgata</s>.
(136) He undertakes effort for the welfare of being reborn in the world of the <s>Buddhas</s>;
he teaches the wholesome <s>Dharma</s> for the benefit of all beings.
(137) Having attained a mind of loving-kindness, devoted to the holy life,
having heard thus, <s>Bhaiṣajyasena</s>, one should be devoted to practice.
(138) The <s>Buddha</s> who shows liberation, whose fame is proclaimed, the guide—
